    bio: 'Ernst Heinrich Weber was born in Wittenberg, Germany, into a family with deep scholarly roots — his father was a professor of theology, and his brothers went on to distinguished scientific careers. He studied medicine at the University of Leipzig, where he would spend virtually his entire academic life, eventually becoming one of its most celebrated professors of anatomy and physiology. His early research focused on the mechanics of touch and the comparative anatomy of the nervous system, but it was his meticulous experiments on sensory discrimination that would secure his permanent place in intellectual history.\n\nIn his landmark 1834 work De Tactu (On Touch) and the expanded 1846 study Der Tastsinn und das Gemeingefühl (The Sense of Touch and Common Sensibility), Weber reported the results of careful experiments on the minimum difference between two stimuli that a human subject could reliably detect. He discovered a striking regularity: the just-noticeable difference between two stimuli was not a fixed absolute quantity but a fixed proportion of the original stimulus. To notice a change in the brightness of a light or the weight of an object, the change had to be a constant fraction of the starting value — roughly one fortieth for weight, one thirtieth for visual length, one tenth for tonal pitch. This elegant proportionality, later formalised mathematically by Gustav Fechner as Weber\'s Law, was the first quantitative relationship ever demonstrated between a physical stimulus and a psychological response.\n\nWeber was a physiologist rather than a philosopher by training and temperament, and he made no grand metaphysical claims about the significance of his findings. But the philosophical implications were profound and immediately recognised by his contemporaries. If the relationship between physical stimulation and conscious sensation obeyed a mathematical law, then consciousness itself was subject to natural regularity — it was, in some sense, a lawful part of the natural world rather than a domain entirely apart from it. This insight provided the empirical foundation on which Gustav Fechner built his visionary programme of psychophysics, and ultimately on which Wundt built the entire enterprise of experimental psychology. Weber\'s modest, meticulous measurements cracked open a door that philosophers and scientists have been arguing about ever since.',
